  "query_preview": "..Upper-air analysis shows the 500mb subtropical high\npressure cell centered across central New Mexico, with a\nnortheastward-moving shortwave trough draped across the Yavapai\nand Coconino County border. This system is actively supporting\nshowers and thunderstorms across western Yavapai and Coconino\nCounties, while aiding convective initiation along portions of the\nMogollon Rim. \n\nModerate monsoon conditions persist today with storms drifting \nnortheastward on average. The forecast favors high-terrain \nlocations, with a chance for showers and thunderstorms between \n30-60% in the mountains. However, mid-level warming is limiting \ndeep atmospheric instability, keeping the threat for severe \nweather low. Given the presence of slow-moving storms, there is a \nlow to medium risk for flash flooding. This threat is tempered by \natmospheric moisture (precipitable water) values ranging from \n0.75-0.85 inches north of the Mogollon Rim and 0.85-1.15 inches \nsouth of the Rim. Outflow wind gusts between 35-45 mph are \npossible in the vicinity of storms. \n\nStorm activity is forecast to decline after sunset. High-\nresolution model guidance indicates overnight showers and\nthunderstorms may move into the region from the south, impacting\nareas west of a Payson to Flagstaff to Page line. Similar\nconvective coverage is forecast on Monday, though a surge of\nmoisture northward from the Gulf of California will likely shift\nthe main axis of high storm coverage into Coconino and Yavapai\nCounties, with more isolated activity expected elsewhere. \n\nBy mid-to-late week, guidance generally agrees that the\nsubtropical high pressure circulation will strengthen over \nArizona. This will introduce more stable atmospheric conditions \nand a noticeable downturn in storm coverage, leaving only low-\ngrade monsoon activity through the remainder of the week. \nTemperatures are forecast to warm during this period, with daytime\ntemperatures approaching 10 degrees above normal across much of \nthe area. Excessive Heat products may become necessary for lower \nelevation areas, including portions of Yavapai County and Grand \nCanyon country.",
